"Create **fig_intercept_error** in **R using ggplot2** as a publication-ready discrete scatter plot titled **\u201cRecorded Mean Squared Error at Three Intercept Corrections\u201d**. Use a clean white background, restrained blue markers, clear sans-serif typography, and a sparse horizontal grid to emphasize the three recorded arithmetic results.\n\nInitialize the complete plotting dataset explicitly as `data.frame(b = c(0, 1.5, 3), mse = c(9, 2.25, 0))`. Map numeric `b` to horizontal position and numeric `mse` to vertical position. Plot exactly **(0, 9)**, **(1.5, 2.25)**, and **(3, 0)**. Use a continuous horizontal scale that preserves the equal spacing between the corrections. Do not include connecting lines, interpolation, fitted curves, uncertainty intervals, additional observations, the five underlying observations, or MAE values.\n\nUse a **9 \u00d7 6 inch landscape canvas**, corresponding to **2700 \u00d7 1800 pixels at 300 dpi** for raster export. Set both plot and panel backgrounds to **#FFFFFF**. Apply outer margins of **18 pt top, 24 pt right, 16 pt bottom, and 18 pt left**. Maintain generous white space around the title, annotations, and caption.\n\nDraw all three markers with `geom_point()`, using **shape 21**, **size 4.2**, and **stroke 0.6** in ggplot2\u2019s native parameter units. Use a constant **#0072B2** fill, **#004A73** outline, and full opacity. Render markers above the grid so their boundaries remain distinct, including the marker at zero MSE. Marker appearance must not encode any additional variable.\n\nLabel the horizontal axis **\u201cAdded intercept correction b\u201d** and the vertical axis **\u201cMean squared error\u201d**. Set horizontal breaks to exactly **0, 1.5, and 3**, displayed as those strings. Set vertical breaks to exactly **0, 2.25, 4.5, 6.75, and 9**, without unnecessary trailing zeros. These vertical ticks are scale references, not additional measurements.\n\nUse horizontal viewing limits of **\u22120.35 to 3.35** and vertical viewing limits of **\u22120.6 to 10.2**, with no additional scale expansion. The padding below zero keeps the zero-error marker fully visible; it does not represent negative MSE observations.\n\nUse **DejaVu Sans** consistently. Render axis titles at **13 pt**, regular weight, in **#222222**, with **10 pt** separating each title from its tick labels. Render tick labels at **11 pt**, regular weight, in **#222222**, keeping all labels horizontal. Draw bottom and left axis lines in **#6B7280** with **0.4 mm** linewidth. Use outward ticks of **3 pt**, colored **#6B7280**, with **0.35 mm** linewidth. Omit top and right panel borders.\n\nAnnotate every marker with its exact coordinate string: **\u201c(0, 9)\u201d**, **\u201c(1.5, 2.25)\u201d**, and **\u201c(3, 0)\u201d**. Center each annotation horizontally over its marker and place it **0.45 vertical data units above** the recorded value, giving text anchors at **(0, 9.45)**, **(1.5, 2.70)**, and **(3, 0.45)**. These offsets apply only to text. Use **12 pt**, regular-weight DejaVu Sans in **#1F2937**. Preserve the exact label strings without rounding or trailing decimal zeros. Convert point-based text sizes appropriately when using ggplot2 geometry parameters.\n\nShow only solid horizontal major gridlines at the specified vertical ticks, using **#E5E7EB** and **0.3 mm** linewidth. Omit vertical gridlines and all minor gridlines. Keep the grid behind the markers and annotations.\n\nRender the exact title in **16 pt bold DejaVu Sans**, colored **#111827**, left-aligned with the plotting panel, with **12 pt** of space below it. Do not display a legend.\n\nPlace the following caption beneath the plot, left-aligned with the plotting panel, in **10 pt regular DejaVu Sans**, colored **#4B5563**, with **12 pt** of separation from the horizontal axis title:\n\n\u201cThree recorded corrections evaluated on the same five synthetic observations used to determine the correction. No stochastic repetitions or held-out evaluation.\u201d\n\nWrap the caption at a natural sentence boundary if needed to keep it within the canvas, preserving its wording exactly and avoiding clipping.\n\nThe finished figure must make the finite comparison directly readable: **MSE is 9 at b = 0, 2.25 at b = 1.5, and 0 at b = 3**. Preserve the discrete presentation and make no claim about unmeasured corrections or predictive generalization."